Chapter: RealLiteralOverflow

 Steve Howell's issue 106. Both literals below are past the width the
 parser can carry. `__text_to_double` folds every digit, on BOTH sides of
 the point, into one wrapping i64 as `acc = acc * 10 + digit`, counts the
 places separately and divides once at the end, so twenty digits wrap
 before the value is ever a float. Before CDX2073 these compiled clean and
 read back as different, negative numbers with no diagnostic: 2 to the 60
 came back through `real-to-int` as -691752902764108160 and 1e18 written
 out as -844674407370955136. It cost him a wrong gold file, because a
 generator wrote a nineteen-digit literal and the oracle lied.

 The width that matters is the TOTAL digit count and not the integer part,
 which is why the sibling test carries a nineteen-digit value that must
 still compile: nineteen digits is the boundary and it is legitimate.

Section: Definitions

  two-to-the-sixty : Real = 1152921504606846976.0

  ten-to-the-eighteen : Real = 1000000000000000000.0

  opening : Text = show two-to-the-sixty & show ten-to-the-eighteen

